/* SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 Daniel Gardiner d/b/a FractalSQLabs
 *
 * Control opcode registry for FSQ protocol v2. X(name, code) per row.
 * Functions are called by name, not by opcode -- see UDF_INIT and UDF_CALL
 * in SPEC.md, and the function registry in functions.def. Opcodes are never
 * reused.
 */

X(PING, 0x0001)
X(VERSION, 0x0002)
/* Lifecycle. The target function's name travels in the payload. */
X(UDF_INIT, 0x0010)
X(UDF_CALL, 0x0011)
X(UDF_DEINIT, 0x0012)
/* Admin-triggered cancel: payload is the handle_id (u64) to cancel. Unlike
 * UDF_CALL/UDF_DEINIT, this is not restricted to the handle's owning
 * connection -- it's meant to be sent from a separate connection (fsqlctl),
 * since the owning connection's thread is the one blocked inside the call
 * being cancelled. Two effects, both cooperative:
 *   - between calls: marks the handle so its *next* UDF_CALL fails with
 *     ERR_BUSY instead of running (never cached; one-shot per CANCEL);
 *   - mid-call: the daemon's in-repo computation loops (parsers, per-element
 *     UDF bodies) poll the same flag at row/element boundaries and abort
 *     with ERR_BUSY too. It does NOT abort work inside the vendored core
 *     archive, and a CANCEL arriving after that call finished does nothing
 *     (one-shot). See SPEC.md's cancel section. */
X(CANCEL, 0x0013)
/* Admin-triggered config reload (fsqlctl reload): payload is empty. The
 * daemon re-parses its config file and swaps it atomically under live
 * connections (allowed lists, timeouts, connection limits, log path).
 * Changes to socket_path or hmac_key_file are refused -- those require a
 * restart. Not the same as a key/config rotation for client-side settings
 * the shim reads once at first call. See SPEC.md's reload section. */
X(RELOAD, 0x0014)
